  "summary": "Jump cuts, street light, and the conviction that a camera is a pen.",
  "description": "The Nouvelle Vague — Truffaut, Godard, Chabrol, Rohmer, Rivette, plus the Left Bank's Resnais and Varda — turned film criticism into film style: location Paris streets, natural light, direct address to camera, jump cuts (Breathless's famous elisions), long takes of conversation, and genre plots hijacked by cinephile reference. Low budgets and fast crews were the aesthetic: Godard shot Breathless with a wheelchair, a tripod and Chabrol's produce-check. The look — black-and-white grain, sunglasses-and-pipe chic, hand-lettered interludes — became the default image of cinematic modernism, and its \"camera-stylo\" freedom licenses every indie auteur since.",
  "culturalContext": "The Nouvelle Vague made the camera a pen: Godard and Truffaut's street-shoot freedom became world cinema's modernist baseline, the look (grain, sunglasses, jump cuts) is the image of cinematic cool, and every indie auteur since inherits their budget-becomes-style logic.",
